This is step 3 (the final part of Phase 1) of the
[reusable eligibility journey plan](eligibility-journey.html).

## Context

Steps 1 and 2 of the eligibility-journey plan ported CCQ's step branching
(`Steps::Logic` + the six section classes → `stepsFor(answers)`) into
`src/eligibility/domain/`. Step 3 is the last piece of Phase 1 (rules engine,
no UI): port CCQ's CFE payload builders so this module can call CFE directly
(D6), and build parity fixtures proving the port is faithful. Everything here
is pinned to the same CCQ commit as the earlier port, `1a30ea3f`
(`~/dev/laa-check-client-qualifies`).

Scope, confirmed with the user:
- **In scope**: `CfeService` + the 10 `Cfe::*PayloadService` classes +
  `CfeParamBuilders::*` → one TS function, `buildAssessment(answers, today)`.
  Plus Mocha ports of their RSpec specs, and parity fixtures built from
  hand-written representative answer sets.
- **Out of scope**: `CfeResult` / `CalculationResult` (interpreting CFE's
  *response*, for the results page). That belongs to the Phase 2/3 UI work,
  not this rules-engine step.
- `buildAssessment` takes an explicit `today: Date` — no implicit
  `new Date()` default — so CCQ's `n.years.ago` / `Time.zone.today` date
  arithmetic stays deterministic for callers and for the parity test.

## File layout

All new files under `src/eligibility/domain/cfe/`, next to the existing
`domain/logic.ts`, `domain/navigation.ts`, `domain/sections/`.

| TS file | Ports | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `payload.ts` | `Cfe::BaseService` + `CfeService.call` | `buildAssessment(answers, today)`: computes `relevantSteps = new Set(stepsFor(answers))` (reusing `stepsFor` from `../navigation.js`), calls every builder below, merges their (disjoint) top-level keys into one payload object. |
| `assessment-section.ts` | `Cfe::AssessmentPayloadService` | needs `today` |
| `applicant.ts` | `Cfe::ApplicantPayloadService` | needs `today` for `submission_date`; reads a real `date_of_birth` answer (no bracket synthesis — see Dates gotcha) |
| `proceedings.ts` | `Cfe::ProceedingsPayloadService` | |
| `assets.ts` | `Cfe::AssetsPayloadService` + `CfeParamBuilders::Capitals` | capitals + properties (main home + additional properties) |
| `dependants.ts` | `Cfe::DependantsPayloadService` + `CfeParamBuilders::Dependants` | |
| `employment-income.ts` | `Cfe::EmploymentIncomePayloadService` + `CfeParamBuilders::Employment` + `::SelfEmployment` | exports a shared `buildEmploymentDetails(incomes)` / `buildSelfEmploymentDetails(incomes)` taking the raw income-row array, so client and partner sections both call it instead of duplicating the frequency table and sign-flipping logic |
| `irregular-income.ts` | `Cfe::IrregularIncomePayloadService` + `CfeParamBuilders::IrregularIncome` | shared builder taking normalised `{studentFinanceRelevant, studentFinanceAmount, otherRelevant, otherAmount, levelOfHelp}`, called once for client fields and once for `partner_`-prefixed fields |
| `regular-transactions.ts` | `Cfe::RegularTransactionsPayloadService` + `CfeParamBuilders::RegularTransactions` | shared builder taking normalised outgoings/other-income/benefits/housing field objects; housing only ever applies to the client call (see gotchas) |
| `vehicles.ts` | `Cfe::VehiclePayloadService` + `CfeParamBuilders::Vehicles` | |
| `partner.ts` | `Cfe::PartnerPayloadService` | composes `assets.ts`'s capitals builder, `employment-income.ts`, `irregular-income.ts`, `regular-transactions.ts`; uses `partnerEmployed` from `../logic.js`; reads a real `partner_date_of_birth` answer (no bracket synthesis — see Dates gotcha) |

No `Check`/form-instantiation layer is ported — CCQ's `Check#method_missing`
and `BaseService#instantiate_form` exist only to read session fields and
validate them; this module already has the flat `answers` bag and
`relevantSteps` (from `stepsFor`), so each builder reads `answers.<field>`
directly, guarded by `relevantSteps.has("<step_code>")` wherever CCQ calls
`relevant_form?(:step_code)`.

## Gotchas found while reading the CCQ source (must carry over)

- **`AdditionalPropertyModel#owned_with_mortgage?`**: for row 0, "owned with
  mortgage" comes from the step-level `additional_property_owned` /
  `partner_additional_property_owned` answer; for every other row it comes
  from that row's own `inline_owned_with_mortgage` field. Needs a small
  shared `resolveOwnedWithMortgage(item, index, ownershipAnswer)` helper, used
  by both the client and partner additional-property row builders (which
  otherwise differ: the partner row never has a `subject_matter_of_dispute`
  key at all, not even `false`).
- **Housing benefit only applies to two of the three housing steps.**
  `MortgageOrLoanPaymentForm` does *not* extend `BaseHousingCostsForm`, so
  `build_housing_benefits` only fires when `housing_costs` or
  `shared_ownership_housing_costs` is the relevant step — never for
  `mortgage_or_loan_payment`. This matches the real-world distinction: housing
  costs/shared ownership steps are for people who rent (housing benefit is a
  rental-support benefit), while mortgage-or-loan-payment is for people who
  own their home outright or are buying it — housing benefit doesn't apply.
- **Housing payment lookup order** in `RegularTransactionsPayloadService` is
  `mortgage_or_loan_payment`, then `shared_ownership_housing_costs`, then
  `housing_costs` (`if`/`elsif` chain) — check in that order.
- **Tax/NI are sign-flipped**: `express_as_negative_figure` negates
  `income_tax` and `national_insurance` for both employment and
  self-employment rows.
- **Dependants' assumed ages** (21 for adults, 17 for children with income,
  11 for children without) and the frequency translation reused from
  `CfeParamBuilders::Employment::FREQUENCY_TRANSLATIONS` — see the comment
  already in `dependants.rb` explaining *why* (CFE can't take "unknown
  age", so these are deliberate stand-ins, not real ages).
- **`other_income` frequency differs by level of help**: `monthly` for
  controlled, `quarterly` for certificated (`irregular_income.rb`).
- **`not_aggregated_no_income_low_capital` is currently unexported** from
  `domain/logic.ts` (it's a private helper at line 148) — needs an `export`
  added since `assessment-section.ts` needs it (port of
  `check.not_aggregated_no_income_low_capital?`).
- **Raw booleans pass straight through**, not via `logic.ts`'s `truthy()`:
  e.g. `controlled_legal_representation` in `assessment-section.ts` is
  assigned as CCQ's form attribute value directly (already a real boolean in
  our session), not re-derived.
- **Dates**: CCQ only ever collects an age *bracket* (`client_age`:
  under_18/over_60/standard, `partner_over_60`: boolean) and fakes a
  `date_of_birth` for CFE via `n.years.ago.to_date`, because CFE only cares
  which bracket a date falls into. This reusable component collects real
  dates of birth, so `applicant.ts` and `partner.ts` read them straight from
  the answers bag instead of synthesizing anything:
  - `applicant.ts` reads a new `date_of_birth` answer field and passes it
    through as-is (formatted `YYYY-MM-DD`) — no `yearsAgo`/bracket logic.
  - `partner.ts` reads a new `partner_date_of_birth` answer field the same
    way.
  - `client_age` / `partner_over_60` keep driving branching logic in
    `logic.ts` (unchanged from steps 1-2); they're just no longer the source
    for the CFE payload's date_of_birth fields.
  - Add both new keys to `STEP_ANSWER_KEYS` (`step-answer-keys.ts`) under
    `client_age` and `partner_details` respectively, alongside the existing
    bracket answers — this is the only change needed outside `cfe/`.
  - `submission_date` is still just `today`, formatted `YYYY-MM-DD`.

## Parity fixtures

CCQ's `instant_controlled_session` / `instant_certificated_session` /
`rich_instant_controlled_session` FactoryBot factories exist only to power
CCQ's dev-only `InstantSessionsController` (a "jump straight into a session"
shortcut for manual QA in that app). That's CCQ-specific test tooling, not
something this reusable component should depend on or carry a reference to.

Instead, hand-write our own representative answer sets directly as plain TS
objects in this repo (not sourced from any CCQ factory), covering the key
branches we need parity on: a controlled-work case, a certificated case, a
passported case, and a case with a partner plus properties/vehicles/
dependants. These live with the fixtures, e.g.
`src/eligibility/domain/cfe/fixtures/*.answers.json`.

Plan:
1. Write a small Ruby script (not run in this repo's CI — CCQ is a separate
   repo) that takes each hand-written answers JSON file as input (plain
   `JSON.parse`, no FactoryBot) and, for each:
   - freezes "today" to a fixed constant date (e.g. `2024-06-01`, matching
     across both the Ruby script and the TS parity test)
   - computes `relevant_steps` via `Steps::Helper.relevant_steps(session_data)`
   - runs the same 10 `Cfe::*PayloadService.call` steps `CfeService.call`
     does, but against a local `payload = {}` hash instead of calling
     `CfeConnection.assess` (same approach `cfe_service_spec.rb` takes, minus
     the mocks)
   - prints `{ input: session_data, relevant_steps:, payload: }` as JSON
2. Run it once with `bin/rails runner` against the pinned CCQ commit, and
   commit the resulting JSON files as
   `src/eligibility/domain/cfe/fixtures/*.json`. Keep the generator script
   alongside them (`fixtures/generate-ccq-fixtures.rb`) with a one-line
   comment on how to re-run it, for when CCQ's pinned commit is bumped or a
   new answer set is added.
3. A Mocha parity test (`domain/cfe/parity.spec.ts`) reads each fixture,
   asserts `stepsFor(fixture.input)` equals `fixture.relevant_steps`, and
   `buildAssessment(fixture.input, FIXED_TODAY)` deep-equals `fixture.payload`.

## Unit tests

Port the existing RSpec files 1:1 into Mocha, same style as
`domain/logic.spec.ts` / `domain/navigation.spec.ts` (plain `describe`/`it` +
node `assert`, not a table-testing library despite the plan doc's wording):

`spec/services/cfe/{applicant,assets,dependants,employment_income,irregular_income,partner,proceedings,regular_transactions,vehicle}_payload_service_spec.rb`
→ one `*.spec.ts` per TS file above, reusing their literal input/output
fixtures.

## Verification

- `yarn test:unit` (Mocha picks up `src/eligibility/**/*.spec.ts` per
  `.mocharc.json`) — all ported unit specs plus the parity test must pass.
- `yarn tsc`/lint as this repo normally runs for TS changes.
- Spot check one fixture by hand: confirm the committed JSON's `payload`
  matches what `bin/rails runner` prints when re-run against CCQ at
  `1a30ea3f` (sanity check the generator script itself is correct before
  trusting the fixtures it produced).
